# Secrets — never commit real environment files. The *.example templates next
# to them ARE committed; note the un-ignore has to be `*.example`, because
# `.env.*.example` needs two dots and would leave `backend/.env.example` out.
.env
.env.*
!*.example

# Dependencies and build output
node_modules/
.next/
out/
build/
dist/
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Go
*.exe
*.test
*.out
/backend/server
/backend/adminreset
/backend/seedmenu
/backend/demodata

# Uploaded images live on the server's disk (a docker volume in production),
# not in the repo — see CLAUDE.md §2. The demo menu's images are embedded in
# the Go binary instead (internal/seed/assets).
/backend/uploads/

# Local tooling / OS / editor noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*.log
.idea/
.vscode/

# The deploy lock, taken at /opt/keel/.deploy.lock on the server. Ignored so a
# checkout that has deployed does not report itself dirty — and so nobody
# "tidies it away" into the repository.
.deploy.lock

# Agent skills installed into this checkout (`npx skills add`, plugin
# marketplaces). ⚠️ **Ignored rather than committed**, because they are
# somebody's tooling and not part of the product: they arrive with megabytes of
# artwork, they update on their own schedule, and a skill that runs with full
# agent permissions is not something to hand every future clone of this
# repository without a decision. A skill the team genuinely wants shared gets
# added on purpose, without the images.
/.claude/skills/
/.agents/
/skills-lock.json

# ---- What the server keeps beside the checkout ----
#
# ⚠️ **Named here so a deploy can never meet them.** The production checkout is
# `/opt/keel`, and these three directories live inside it: the signing keys, the
# artifacts they sign, and the service accounts. Left untracked-but-not-ignored
# they show up in `git status`, and the day a commit happens to add a path under
# one of them, `git reset --hard` refuses — which is a deploy that stops halfway
# with the working tree already half updated. That is not hypothetical: it is
# what happened the first time this app's build pipeline was deployed.
#
# ⚠️ **`appkeys/` is the one that must never be deleted, ever.** An app on the
# store signed by a lost key can never be updated — not by us, not by the
# restaurant, not by Google. Ignoring it here also keeps it out of reach of a
# `git clean -fd`, which is the command somebody reaches for when a deploy is
# stuck.
/appkeys/
/appbuilds/
/secrets/
